  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rational\n   The provided program uses simulated annealing with resolution upgrades between levels. The core idea is to find a step function that minimizes the maximum overlap with its shifts. However, two-level optimization with annealing may not be sufficient to find the global minimum, making it prone to local optima. The new approach will use a multi-level strategy with three phases:\n\n   - **Parent Transfer**: Interpolate the initial parent (from `initial_h_values`) to the next level's resolution using linear interpolation.\n   - **Stochastic Search**: Apply a specialized stochastic optimization algorithm (Simulated Annealing with adaptive neighborhood exploration) that focuses on boundary maintenance and diverse move probabilities.\n   - **Local Optimization**: For each level, first find a good initial candidate using the stochastic search, then perform gradient-based refinement using a repaired gradient function.\n\n   This method improves upon the parent by using persistent repair mechanisms and better transition strategies between levels.\n\n   Key improvements:\n   - Enhanced repair function that maintains 0\u2264h[i]1 while adjusting sum exactly.\n   - Adaptive neighborhood exploration in the stochastic search to balance diversity and focus.\n   - Gradient-based local optimization using forward and backward derivatives of the overlap integral.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   The objective minimizes:\n     C5 = max_k dx * \u222b h(x)[1-h(x+k)] dx\n   Constraints:\n     0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1, dx=2/n_points, and \u222bdx h(x)=1 implies `sum(h) = n/2`\n\n   Representation:\n     h(x) is represented as a NumPy array of values at uniformly spaced points.\n\n   Optimization:\n     We minimize the maximum correlation value via:\n        corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n        C5 = dx * max(corr)\n\n   The repair function uses iterative clipping and scaling to enforce:\n        0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n        sum(h) = n/2 (exactly)\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   The procedure has six main sections:\n\n   Phase 1: Initialization and Setup\n     a. Extract n0 from the initial_h_values (n=95) if available, else use a fallback uniform function.\n     b. Set up timing and resource monitoring.\n     c. Define configuration parameters for stochastic and local search phases.\n\n   Phase 2: Objective and Repair Functions\n     d. Define a repair function that:\n        - Clips values to [0,1]\n        - Iteratively scales to achieve sum exactly equal to n/2\n        - Includes a fallback scaling method for cases where clipping prevents integral adjustment\n     e. Implement the objective function to: (1) repair input, (2) compute maximum correlation, (3) return C5*dx\n\n   Phase 3: Stochastic Optimization\n     f. Initialize a temperature-scheduled annealing schedule using the parent from the previous level.\n     g. For each temperature stage:\n        - Explore moves with probability based on acceptance using adaptive delta range.\n        - Prefer boundary exploration near shifts that yield higher overlap.\n        - Maintain a diversity pool to avoid premature convergence.\n     h. Convert the best candidate to local optimization format.\n\n   Phase 4: Local Optimization\n     i. Use gradient descent with line search, computing gradients via finite-differences.\n     j. Implement specialized loss functions for the maximum operation by considering top correlations.\n\n   Phase 5: Resolution Upgrades\n     k. Set target resolution levels based on budget allocation proportionally.\n     l. Interpolate parents preserving the function shape.\n     m. Repair and optimize candidates at each new resolution level.\n\n   Phase 6: Selection and ReturnValue\n     n. Track the best C5 value found across all levels.\n     o. After time expires, return the best candidate found and its score.\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations\n   Configuration Defaults:\n     Temperature schedule: Linearly decayed over iterations.\n     Move probabilities: Adaptive based on delta value and boundary positions.\n     Stochastic search: 10,000 iterations per level (proportional to resolution)\n     Local search: 10,000 iterations per level with adaptive learning rate.\n\n   Computed Repairs:\n     The repair function is defined by iterating clipping and scaling while checking for exact integral match.\n\n   5-level optimization plan: [95, 380, 760, 1520, 3040]\n\n5. Numerical Robustness and Memory Use\n   Memory: \n     - Store only the current/repaired candidate vector and correlation values.\n     - Handle large n_levels by progressive interpolation (linear) which uses O(n) memory.\n\n   Fallbacks:\n     - If repair fails to meet integral constraint, repair using a log-sum (fallback repair method).\n     - If optimizer stalls, revert to the midpoint of the best and worst exploration samples.\n\n6. Runtime Safeguards and Return Contract\n   Time Management:\n     - Allocate 60% of time to initial search, 20% per resolution level.\n     - Fallback if time estimate exceeds budget: reduce level count.\n\n   Feasibility Enforcement:\n     - Repair before every evaluation.\n     - Reject candidates with NaNs or negative overlaps.\n\n   Return contract:\n     Stores best_candidate and its C5 value separately to avoid influence of repairs.\\",
